        { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Ferdinand de Saussure, the founder of modern structural linguistics, represents for Bakhtin the paradigmatic error of treating language as an abstract system rather than a living social event. Saussure\'s great contribution was to analyze language as a self-contained structure of differences — a system in which signs derive their meaning not from any inherent connection to the world but from their relations to other signs within the system. This was enormously productive for linguistics, semiotics, and eventually the broader structuralist movement in anthropology, psychology, and literary theory. But Bakhtin believed it purchased its analytical power at the cost of everything that actually matters about language.' },
        { type: 'paragraph', text: 'The Saussurean model, Bakhtin argued, treats language as if it were a code that speakers simply encode and decode — a shared system that precedes any individual act of speaking and constrains it from outside. The living utterance, with its specific addressee, its evaluative accent, its responsiveness to what has been said and its anticipation of what will be said, simply disappears from this picture. What remains is an elegant formal structure from which the speaker, the listener, the historical situation, and the social struggle over meaning have all been methodologically expelled. For Bakhtin, this is not a simplification but a falsification: you cannot understand what language *is* by studying it in the absence of the very thing that makes it language — its dialogic life.' }
